The issue is with the last condition in the test method RemoveContract. I'm not sure how to do the verification part as I do not know the expected index position of the last occurrence of 'X' after the removal operation. 

A: The Pex generated test is not designed to include the last occurrence index of x in the list after the operation. This is because Pex cannot predict the last index after a removal operation, which requires knowledge that is not predictable based on the input.
You can workaround this issue by simply adding a constraint on the last index to be less than or equal to the original count but if you need to ensure that x is removed then you should consider splitting your test into two: one for the add operation and one for the remove operation. This way you can make Pex predict which values are inserted and at which position so the remove operation can be tested correctly.
If you cannot split your tests due to limitations then you'd have to write a complex assertion function to check the last index of x after the operation. This is not ideal but could be done. However remember that Pex might not catch all possible incorrect states.
Hope this helps,
Best Regards,
Anu 

A: The issue I see is that you are checking after the removal if the last index of the removed value (x) is less than or equal to the new size of the array which makes sense logically but Pex is not predicting the value of the index after the operation is performed.

The Pex tool has limitations with predicting the outcome of an operation like
  removal of an element which can't be predicted easily by its position value
  in the array list because the elements shift positions after removal i.e.,
  the old last position will now contain a new element. The tool can only
  check the count and the element presence/absence in the ArrayList. 

So if you want to make a verification post removal operation, you may consider breaking down your test into two test methods - one for Add() and another for Remove() and check the desired conditions. 
Please let me know if you need any further assistance.

P.S.: The Pex tool will keep adding more and more complex path conditions as it continues to explore the code. So, by increasing the number of path conditions for Remove() call, you should be able to achieve your desired verification condition.

Hope this helps,
Best Regards,
Anil 

A: The PEX tool is not designed for scenarios where you need to inspect the values of the objects after invocation. It's just like checking for properties of objects after operations, for instance. 
In order to accomplish your goal, you can simply create a method to handle all the necessary assertions. It can be something like:

[PexMethod]
public void PUT_RemoveContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));

    int oldCount = arrList.Count;
    arrList.Remove(x);

    CustomAssertions(arrList, oldCount, x);
}

private void CustomAssertions(ArrayList arrList, int oldCount, int x)
{
    // Assert that the Count property has decreased by 1.
    Assert.AreEqual(oldCount - 1, arrList.Count);

    // Assert that x is not contained in the ArrayList.
    Assert.IsFalse(arrList.Contains(x));

    // Assert that the LastIndexOf method returns -1.
    Assert.AreEqual(-1, arrList.LastIndexOf(x));
}

This method has its own tests and you do not have to change anything in the main test and only have to implement a different helper for the specific needs. Be aware this also duplicates some assertions from the original test, you might want to handle these as you see fit.

An alternative way would be to consider splitting PUT_RemoveContract tests into two: to verify that element is removed, and post-remove condition checks:

[PexMethod]
public void PUT_RemoveContract_RemoveElem([PexAssumeUnderTest] IList<int> arrList, int x)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));

    arrList.Remove(x);

    ... // check conditions for Remove operation - count etc.
}

[PexMethod]
public void PUT_RemoveContract_PostRemove([PexAssumeUnderTest]--------------------	}
   }
}

